06:10:35,342 --> 06:10:39,332
And what kind of information Amazon has about this 100 million customer?

5689
06:10:39,950 --> 06:10:57,452
These information like the name of the customer, address of the customer, gender of the customer, from what date they are customer on Amazon, what purchases they have made in the last three months, one month, six months, one year, and other things they know about uh these customer.

5690
06:10:58,126 --> 06:11:02,340
So these are all the information which the Amazon would have about these customers.

5691
06:11:02,544 --> 06:11:04,580
For each customer, all these information.

5692
06:11:05,678 --> 06:11:07,844
Now, in this case, all the columns are input.

5693
06:11:08,430 --> 06:11:10,944
We are not trying to predict anything in this case.

5694
06:11:11,104 --> 06:11:18,384
All of these are just inputs, the features of a customer or like the details related to a customer.

5695
06:11:18,544 --> 06:11:19,396
These all things.

5696
06:11:19,726 --> 06:11:20,292
And right.

5697
06:11:20,558 --> 06:11:24,132
And these are known as the input columns, which we have to use.

5698
06:11:25,230 --> 06:11:26,628
But what kind of task do we do?

5699
06:11:27,504 --> 06:11:31,460
So let's say that we have detail about 100 million customers.

5700
06:11:31,824 --> 06:11:34,212
We have to do what in unsupervised learning?

5701
06:11:34,624 --> 06:11:42,404
We have to divide all the data points, or we have to divide all the customers into various groups, segments, and clusters.

5702
06:11:43,022 --> 06:11:43,344
Correct.

5703
06:11:43,694 --> 06:11:48,292
So either you say groups or you say segments or you say cluster, it means the same thing.

5704
06:11:49,134 --> 06:11:57,264
So our main task in unsupervised learning is to take the data and divide that among multiple groups.

5705
06:11:57,550 --> 06:12:00,612
So currently we have the entire data somewhere together.

5706
06:12:01,134 --> 06:12:05,764
And we have to first identify that how many groups exist in our data set.

5707
06:12:06,638 --> 06:12:08,384
So first we have to identify that.

5708
06:12:08,718 --> 06:12:13,380
Then we have to divide our data, entire data, into that many groups.

5709
06:12:13,998 --> 06:12:18,468
So that will be our major main task or main focus in unsupervised learning.

5710
06:12:19,118 --> 06:12:29,188
Now, when you're dividing all the customers into various groups or segments or clusters, the groups will be based upon some similarities between the customers or the data point.

5711
06:12:29,774 --> 06:12:35,940
Here every row is a customer detail, and you can call every customer as a data point.

5712
06:12:36,590 --> 06:12:36,944
Correct.

5713
06:12:37,166 --> 06:12:42,564
Because when you make a graph, if you make a dot, this dot is representing a row always.

5714
06:12:43,182 --> 06:12:45,412
So in every row, I have a customer detail.

5715
06:12:45,678 --> 06:12:49,060
So every dot is a customer or a data point, anything you can say.

5716
06:12:49,486 --> 06:12:50,180
Right now.

5717
06:12:50,624 --> 06:12:58,144
So when I divide the customers into multiple groups, in each group you will have similar type of data point.

5718
06:12:58,624 --> 06:13:01,764
You will not put dissimilar data points into the same group.

5719
06:13:02,094 --> 06:13:03,236
That is not our task.

5720
06:13:03,630 --> 06:13:14,148
So we have to put if I'm putting let's say one million customers into one group, so it means that those 1 million customers are somehow similar to each other.

5721
06:13:14,734 --> 06:13:21,744
Either their gender is same, their age is same, their city is same, their country is same, right?

5722
06:13:22,126 --> 06:13:29,124
So some sort of similarity must be there in the customers on the basis of which you are putting them into one group.

5723
06:13:30,144 --> 06:13:30,464
Right.

5724
06:13:30,624 --> 06:13:38,144
So again, we have data about 100 million customers, and we have these information about every customer.

5725
06:13:38,286 --> 06:13:41,316
So 100 million rows are there, 100 million data points are there.

5726
06:13:42,062 --> 06:13:48,100
So first we have to divide the customers into various groups and, or you can say clusters.

5727
06:13:48,430 --> 06:13:52,228
Clusters is the main term we will be using, which means nothing but groups.

5728
06:13:52,704 --> 06:14:00,036
So we will divide the customers into multiple clusters or groups, and each group will have similar data points to each other.

5729
06:14:00,718 --> 06:14:01,344
Right?

5730
06:14:02,126 --> 06:14:05,700
And there is one very important property you should know about these clusters.

5731
06:14:06,222 --> 06:14:10,596
All the data points within one group should be very, very similar to each other.

5732
06:14:11,598 --> 06:14:11,904
Correct.

5733
06:14:12,110 --> 06:14:17,380
The data points which are within a group should be as similar to each other as possible.

5734
06:14:18,030 --> 06:14:26,660
So if I have made one group and if I put some customers here into this group, so these all the customers should be very, very similar to each other.

5735
06:14:27,278 --> 06:14:27,716
Correct.

5736
06:14:28,174 --> 06:14:31,140
So we'll be talking about what do I mean by similarity.

5737
06:14:31,664 --> 06:14:36,324
But these customers should be similar to each other in some way, very, very similar.

5738
06:14:36,944 --> 06:14:40,676
And if I have another group, and in this also some data points are there.

5739
06:14:41,230 --> 06:14:44,068
So these data points should be very similar to each other.

5740
06:14:44,398 --> 06:14:52,740
But if I pick one data point from this and one data point from this, then they should be as dissimilar to each other as possible.

5741
06:14:53,390 --> 06:14:59,268
The higher the dissimilarity between them, the more stable the group we have identified.

5742
06:14:59,918 --> 06:15:00,356
Correct.

5743
06:15:00,718 --> 06:15:08,260
So there are two properties we have to follow, and on the basis of that, we will be able to identify correct number of groups.

5744
06:15:08,782 --> 06:15:16,932
So if I have 100 million data points, I can make 10 groups, 100 groups, 1000 groups, even 10,000 groups.

5745
06:15:17,486 --> 06:15:19,364
But how much groups are enough?

5746
06:15:19,982 --> 06:15:20,304
Right?

5747
06:15:20,544 --> 06:15:24,356
So that will be decided on the basis of these two factors.

5748
06:15:25,230 --> 06:15:30,532
That in all the data points within a group should be as similar to each other as possible.

5749
06:15:30,720 --> 06:15:38,500
At the same time, a data point from one group should be as dissimilar to a data point from another group as much as possible.

5750
06:15:39,054 --> 06:15:46,724
Both of the factors have to be followed at the same time, and then only we can make more stable clusters in our data set.

5751
06:15:47,470 --> 06:15:47,908
Correct.

5752
06:15:48,960 --> 06:15:52,644
Now, what kind of similarity am I talking about?

5753
06:15:53,070 --> 06:15:58,564
So Amazon can make one group where we have young customers who like to spend high.

5754
06:15:59,120 --> 06:15:59,440
Right?

5755
06:15:59,520 --> 06:16:04,612
They spend too much on Amazon and they are young customers, like till the age of 25, let's say.

5756
06:16:05,518 --> 06:16:11,428
Then we can have another group where we have young customers, but they spend less.

5757
06:16:11,822 --> 06:16:14,308
They don't spend anything above 1000 rupees.

5758
06:16:14,960 --> 06:16:16,640
they don't like to spend much.

5759
06:16:16,910 --> 06:16:17,316
Correct.

5760
06:16:17,680 --> 06:16:22,948
So in this group, we can have all the customers who are young but spend less.

5761
06:16:23,598 --> 06:16:28,960
Then we can have another group where we can have mid-age people and they spend less.

5762
06:16:29,040 --> 06:16:32,676
Then we can have another group where we have mid-age people who spend high.

5763
06:16:33,038 --> 06:16:37,828
Then we can have old customers who spend less and old customers who spend high.

5764
06:16:38,478 --> 06:16:40,548
And here I'm just considering two factors.

5765
06:16:41,134 --> 06:16:41,572
Right?

5766
06:16:41,870 --> 06:16:45,444
That they are young and they like to spend a certain amount.

5767
06:16:45,870 --> 06:16:49,156
Only two factors I'm just considering to make these multiple groups.

5768
06:16:49,520 --> 06:17:01,284
If I consider third factor, four factors, fifth factor, then the quality of the groups will become better and better if I take more and more of these factors.

5769
06:17:01,646 --> 06:17:03,876
But the factors which I take should be good.

5770
06:17:04,462 --> 06:17:05,796
And what do I mean by that?

5771
06:17:06,318 --> 06:17:07,876
Let me show you that with an example.

5772
06:17:08,494 --> 06:17:10,116
They have to be dissimilar.

5773
06:17:10,542 --> 06:17:13,668
First group and second group have to be dissimilar in some way.

5774
06:17:14,094 --> 06:17:15,716
Some way they have to be dissimilar.

5775
06:17:16,014 --> 06:17:20,388
So these both are young people, but these are high spenders and these are low spenders.

5776
06:17:20,814 --> 06:17:22,960
So that makes them different, right?

5777
06:17:23,360 --> 06:17:25,444
That is what that makes them separate.

5778
06:17:25,902 --> 06:17:31,140
So one data point from this group and one data point from this group should be very dissimilar from each other.

5779
06:17:31,406 --> 06:17:32,932
That is what we are trying to make sure.

5780
06:17:33,486 --> 06:17:39,140
So let us see that what could be the factors which are better for making the clusters.

5781
06:17:39,854 --> 06:17:41,476
Okay, let's have a look at that.

5782
06:17:42,000 --> 06:17:52,836
So let's say another example that I have Intellipad, and thousand learners come to IntelliPad for enrolling into the data science course.

5783
06:17:53,934 --> 06:18:03,652
Now, Intellipad once receives 1000 applications for data science course, they have to divide these thousand people into different batches.

5784
06:18:04,654 --> 06:18:04,960
Right.

5785
06:18:05,280 --> 06:18:09,476
One thing they can do is they can put all the thousand uh students in one batch.

5786
06:18:10,030 --> 06:18:13,680
But the problem will be that it will not be manageable for Intellipad also.

5787
06:18:14,382 --> 06:18:19,204
It will be not manageable by the instructor also to handle 1,000 people in one batch.

5788
06:18:19,854 --> 06:18:27,460
So whenever we have a certain amount of data, we have to see if I put everything into one group, will it be manageable?

5789
06:18:28,110 --> 06:18:35,040
And so that way, if Amazon put all the 100 million customers in one group, is it manageable by them?

5790
06:18:35,342 --> 06:18:42,560
So they'll say no, it is not manageable because there are too many data points and they have a lot of dissimilarity among them.

5791
06:18:42,734 --> 06:18:46,724
So we don't know what to do with that data since they are so much dissimilar.

5792
06:18:47,310 --> 06:18:54,788
So we have to divide the data points into multiple groups so that we can serve them better as a company.

5793
06:18:55,726 --> 06:19:03,364
So, one thing the Intellipad did, they thought that let's divide the customers on the basis of gender.

5794
06:19:04,206 --> 06:19:15,172
For example, let's say they said 500 learners we will put into one group or one batch, and 500 we will put into another batch, all the female learners.

5795
06:19:15,360 --> 06:19:20,240
Right now, is this going to make a good quality cluster for them?

5796
06:19:20,400 --> 06:19:25,280
Is it going to be any help to that company or anywhere?

5797
06:19:25,440 --> 06:19:27,120
Is it going to be of any help?

5798
06:19:27,280 --> 06:19:27,812
Not at all.

5799
06:19:28,398 --> 06:19:28,720
Right.

5800
06:19:28,960 --> 06:19:38,596
Because either you are putting them, those thousand people into one batch, or you are dividing them into on the basis of gender into two batches, it is not going to be helpful at all.

5801
06:19:39,120 --> 06:19:47,684
This one factor alone does seem to be a very bad factor to divide these thousand data points into two groups.

5802
06:19:48,526 --> 06:19:49,028
Correct.

5803
06:19:49,582 --> 06:19:56,260
Let's say if I take another factor, let's say we don't use the gender, rather, we use the domain of the learners.

5804
06:19:56,974 --> 06:20:07,684
Now, if I change this this factor from the gender to this domain, will that be a much better factor to divide these learners into groups?

5805
06:20:08,398 --> 06:20:09,840
Will it be a much better factor?

5806
06:20:10,000 --> 06:20:21,764
Of course, it will be a much better factor because now, if thousand learners are divided into batches of IT, those who belong to IT domain and those who not who belong to non-IT domain.

5807
06:20:22,160 --> 06:20:34,404
then in that case, Intellipad will be able to serve the learners in a better way because they know how these learners have to be taught and how these learners have to be taught, how much technical things they know on the basis of that.

5808
06:20:35,086 --> 06:20:43,364
So, what I'm trying to show you here that there can be multiple factors on the basis of which you can make groups, but all the factors are not good enough.

5809
06:20:44,080 --> 06:20:50,052
Like in the supervised learning, all the input columns are not equally important to predict the output.

5810
06:20:50,240 --> 06:20:54,116
There will be some very important columns and there will be some very less important.

5811
06:20:54,734 --> 06:21:01,860
Same way, we can have multiple factors, and every factor will not be equally important for making the groups.

5812
06:21:02,254 --> 06:21:05,444
Some will be very much important, some will be very less important.

5813
06:21:07,054 --> 06:21:11,044
If instead of taking single single factor, what if I combine two into one?

5814
06:21:11,598 --> 06:21:15,716
Let's say I took IT non-IT, that gave me very good cluster.

5815
06:21:15,920 --> 06:21:18,628
But what if I take two of the factors?

5816
06:21:18,990 --> 06:21:21,540
Let's say their experience level and domain.

5817
06:21:22,318 --> 06:21:31,076
So one group I make fresher IT, fresher non-IT, experience of IT domain, experience learners from non-IT domain.

5818
06:21:31,680 --> 06:21:43,940
If I have these four groups on the basis of these two factors, I can see the quality of the group increasing much just because of taking these two factors, which are very good factors of making the groups.

5819
06:21:44,686 --> 06:21:55,204
And that way you are understanding that whenever you have to do this kind of unsupervised learning task, then all the factors you don't have to take.

5820
06:21:55,534 --> 06:22:06,180
You have to clearly understand out of given, let's say, 50 input columns, 50 input factors, which of the input factors are good enough to make the groups.

5821
06:22:06,574 --> 06:22:17,060
So out of 50 input columns, you can just use 10 of them or 12 of them, which seems to be the most important one, and you can discard totally the rest of them for making the final groups.

5822
06:22:17,838 --> 06:22:18,240
Right.

5823
06:22:18,510 --> 06:22:21,956
And this is an ongoing experimental process.

5824
06:22:22,670 --> 06:22:29,668
In this case, it is very difficult to identify those 10, 12 input columns, which will be very helpful for you.

5825
06:22:30,000 --> 06:22:36,164
So this kind of task is not solved in let's say two months, three months, four months.

5826
06:22:36,480 --> 06:22:38,852
This kind of task goes on for years.

5827
06:22:39,534 --> 06:22:43,364
So you will be trying 10 first, then you try that for six months.

5828
06:22:43,662 --> 06:22:46,756
Then you pick another 10 features, you try that for another six months.

5829
06:22:47,246 --> 06:22:56,240
So after multiple trying, you will be finding out those 10, 12, or 15 features that were giving you the best result.

5830
06:22:56,622 --> 06:22:56,880
Right.

5831
06:22:57,230 --> 06:23:05,380
When you divided the customers into that many groups, taking those factors, you were able to make very good quality groups.

5832
06:23:05,838 --> 06:23:09,988
And what we do by making those good quality groups, what is the benefit?

5833
06:23:10,382 --> 06:23:14,880
First, the benefit is that a company will be able to um right.

5834
06:23:15,120 --> 06:23:21,840
A company will be able to serve those customers better if they have made good quality clusters, right?

5835
06:23:22,080 --> 06:23:30,240
And Second, you see that if the Amazon has divided the customers into multiple groups, right?

5836
06:23:30,670 --> 06:23:36,452
They know that one group is that kind of people who are young but less spenders.

5837
06:23:36,910 --> 06:23:40,260
And one group is those customers who are young but high spenders.

5838
06:23:40,654 --> 06:23:44,964
So Amazon can do a very proper targeted marketing.

5839
06:23:45,326 --> 06:23:50,852
Any company can do a proper targeted marketing on the basis of those things that they know.

5840
06:23:51,598 --> 06:23:53,060
Targeted marketing was what?

5841
06:23:53,440 --> 06:23:59,172
If let's say Amazon has some sale going on or they have any offer going on.

5842
06:23:59,840 --> 06:24:06,340
Now they have to send notification about that offer or sale to some customers.

5843
06:24:07,054 --> 06:24:16,900
Now, whenever you have your Amazon app installed, you will be getting a lot of notifications of Flipkart, Amazon, any any of these applications that you have installed.

5844
06:24:17,120 --> 06:24:22,308
Any means any startup who is selling some, let's say, beauty products or health-related products.

5845
06:24:22,606 --> 06:24:27,044
That also, if you have installed, you'll be getting notifications about the offer on your smartphone.

5846
06:24:27,886 --> 06:24:32,900
Now, the same notification, same offer is not being sent to all the customers of that.

5847
06:24:33,742 --> 06:24:40,960
You are being sent a very specific type of notification, which makes you spend more, right?

5848
06:24:41,120 --> 06:24:46,372
Which makes you purchase that product which is being sold to you.

5849
06:24:47,118 --> 06:24:47,520
Right.

5850
06:24:47,726 --> 06:24:50,180
And this is known as a targeted marketing.

5851
06:24:50,720 --> 06:24:57,252
So if those companies know what kind of customer you are, then they will be able to target you better.

5852
06:24:57,550 --> 06:25:01,600
So all the people who are in this group, they are all young and high spenders.

5853
06:25:01,806 --> 06:25:03,204
They like to spend high, they are young.

5854
06:25:03,534 --> 06:25:10,852
So they have to be sent those kind of notifications which will make them purchase those kind of products.

5855
06:25:11,246 --> 06:25:22,320
So about any fashion product which is coming up, about any new smartphone which is coming up, and they are the people who like those kind of products and they're capable of spending that much.

5856
06:25:22,638 --> 06:25:22,880
Correct.

5857
06:25:22,960 --> 06:25:27,780
So they will be sent those notifications which will make these people purchase that item.

5858
06:25:28,622 --> 06:25:35,108
So targeted marketing is one of the most famous kind of application of unsupervised learning.

5859
06:25:35,726 --> 06:25:41,508
So first the companies will divide all the customers into group, then they will be serving them in different ways.

5860
06:25:42,670 --> 06:25:48,996
Second, the second very famous example of this unsupervised learning is recommendation systems.

5861
06:25:49,550 --> 06:25:52,580
Recommendation systems is a very famous thing now.

5862
06:25:52,880 --> 06:26:00,388
It is there in the market from last 10 years and it has improved to a very good extent as of now.

5863
06:26:01,166 --> 06:26:02,640
The recommendation systems are what?

5864
06:26:02,880 --> 06:26:13,860
When you go on any website and you're looking for a product, as you scroll down on the same page of that product, you'll see other items being recommended to you, which are similar to that product.

5865
06:26:14,670 --> 06:26:17,200
So, how something is being recommended to you, right?

5866
06:26:17,280 --> 06:26:18,084
On what basis?

5867
06:26:18,894 --> 06:26:23,920
So there are two types of recommendation systems that work in the real world market.

5868
06:26:24,142 --> 06:26:28,164
Two types: collaborative filtering, content-based filtering.

5869
06:26:28,480 --> 06:26:28,880
Correct.

5870
06:26:28,960 --> 06:26:33,284
And you will be making uh, I think, a project of this recommendation system as you move forward.

5871
06:26:34,080 --> 06:26:38,372
So, recommendation systems are of two types collaborative, content-based.

5872
06:26:38,560 --> 06:26:46,372
And recommendation systems are an application of unsupervised learning only that you have been using on a day-to-day basis till now.

5873
06:26:47,534 --> 06:26:49,092
So, what is collaborative filtering?

5874
06:26:49,600 --> 06:26:52,580
In collaborative filtering, this kind of thing happens.

5875
06:26:53,198 --> 06:27:00,100
Let's say that this is person one, this is person two, and these both people are in the same group because these are similar type of customers.

5876
06:27:00,654 --> 06:27:01,668
The company knows that.

5877
06:27:02,478 --> 06:27:04,964
That these are both the customers in the same group.

5878
06:27:05,600 --> 06:27:14,436
And now let's say this person one purchases a t shirt from that company Amazon, Flipkart, whatever, Walmart, anything.

5879
06:27:15,566 --> 06:27:20,320
So let's say this person goes onto that platform, purchases a t shirt.

5880
06:27:21,582 --> 06:27:26,436
Now, the company knows that this person is also very similar type of customer to this.

5881
06:27:27,150 --> 06:27:33,856
So, this person too will also be recommended this t-shirt on their home page when they open that application.

5882
06:27:34,016 --> 06:27:36,516
Somewhere they will be recommended this item.

5883
06:27:37,262 --> 06:27:47,076
And why this item is being recommended to this person in the hope that since these are similar type of customers, they might have similar type of likes somewhat or the other.

5884
06:27:47,758 --> 06:28:00,772
And this person too will be more likely to purchase this t-shirt compared to any other t-shirt if we recommend them, or compared to any other product if we recommend them, there are low chances that this person might like them.

5885
06:28:01,198 --> 06:28:04,196
Because we don't know what is the actual liking of this person.

5886
06:28:04,846 --> 06:28:15,056
But if person one likes this t-shirt, the same t-shirt, if that is being recommended to person two, there are very high chances that this person two will purchase that.

5887
06:28:15,566 --> 06:28:26,116
Now, in this group, there will be other people also person three, person four, person five, lot of other customers are there, thousands, even millions of customers are there in a group.

5888
06:28:26,510 --> 06:28:34,852
So if you are being recommended any type of product, one, it can be on the basis of your search history, what kind of thing you like.

5889
06:28:35,342 --> 06:28:45,252
